  "answer": "Mitosis and meiosis are two types of cell division, but they have several key differences. Mitosis occurs in all body cells and results in two daughter cells that are genetically identical to the parent cell. It retains the same number of chromosomes (2n). Meiosis, on the other hand, occurs only in sex cells (gametes) and results in four daughter cells that have half the number of chromosomes (n). This is called reduction division. Mitosis is a single process, while meiosis consists of two phases. Additionally, meiosis involves the pairing and exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes, which does not occur in mitosis. These differences are important for understanding how organisms grow, repair, and reproduce.",
